{
  "abstract": "This paper studies Diophantine septuples over the function field of a genus-one curve, with k constant entries and 7 − k moving entries. Section 2 maps the genus-one architectures treated in earlier rStation papers by (k, 7 − k) and by how the moving entries are linked. Each row gives its status over Q and over number fields, with sources; the rows were sent to their authors for correction. The new row is (1,6): one constant a and six translates h(P + kT) of one function on E_c, with T of order 6 and h = (u² − 1)/a. For u of degree 2 there are two components, called −31 and −23. Each is an elliptic curve of septuples over a totally complex field K of degree 24 [exact]. codex_arithmetic certified the −31 component and proved that its image has genus one. The −23 component and its genus-one check are the author's and have not been replayed independently. On each component E_c(K) has a point of infinite order, so each gives infinitely many septuples with entries in K; explicit ones are verified [exact]. On both, E_c has full 2-torsion over Q(c), the constant a is strong (1 + a² is a square), and translation by a 2-torsion point outside ⟨T⟩ acts on every moving entry as the reflection x ↦ (a − x)/(1 + ax). The field contains √−1, which enters through the coefficients of u and not through c or a [exact]; whether it is forced is open. Over Q, on the reflection-symmetric locus, msolve elimination gives exactly the two known cubics, neither with a rational root [multimodular, probabilistic]. Off that locus, no nondegenerate degree-2 source has good reduction at 41 or 71 [mod p]. Exactly verified positive controls are included. Nothing here excludes an isolated rational septuple.",
